From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bayles <jophba@chromium.org>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:30:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] flac: Support 33-bit Side channels in 32-bit stereo streams

For Left/Side, Mid/Side, and Right/Side stereo decorrelation, the FLAC
specification (RFC 9639, sections 4.2 and 9.2.3) requires the Side
channel to be coded with one extra bit per sample. For 32-bit streams,
the Side channel therefore requires 33 bits per sample, which cannot be
represented by an i32.

Previously, the decoder read the 33-bit Side subframe into the i32
sample buffer. As a result:
 - Warm-up, verbatim, and constant samples were read with
   read_bits_leq32(33), which (with overflow checks disabled) silently
   discards the leading bits, corrupting the predictor warm-up.
 - LPC prediction and Mid/Side reconstruction on 33-bit values are not
   modular, so truncation to i32 produced incorrect samples.
The corruption propagates through the IIR predictor for the remainder of
each block, producing loud bursts of static.

This patch decodes the Side channel into a separate i64 scratch buffer
when the frame is 32 bits per sample, using 64-bit verbatim, fixed, and
LPC decoding paths (similar to libFLAC and FFmpeg), and then restores
the Left and Right channels from it. Streams of 31 bits per sample or
less are unaffected and continue to use the existing i32 path.

Additionally, the i32 LPC predictor now uses wrapping addition, since for
32-bit streams the truncated prediction may legitimately overflow before
the residual is applied.

Note: the 64-bit paths intentionally duplicate the existing i32 subframe
decoders rather than refactoring them, to minimize the patch delta and
leave the existing (<= 31 bits per sample) decoding paths untouched. For
the upstream change, we plan to instead make the subframe decoders
generic over i32/i64.
Upstream issue: https://github.com/pdeljanov/Symphonia/issues/585
Upstream PR: https://github.com/pdeljanov/Symphonia/pull/587
Chromium bug: https://issues.chromium.org/issues/572363272

